==English==

{{webster}}

===Etymology===
{{etyl|ang}} ''[[hidous]]'', {{etyl|fro}} ''[[hidous]], [[hidos]], [[hidus]], [[hisdos]], [[hisdous]]'', {{etyl|fr}} ''[[hideux]]'': confer {{etyl|fro}} ''[[hide]]'', {{term|hisde||fright}}; of uncertain origin; confer {{etyl|goh}} {{term|egidī||horror}}, or {{etyl|la}} ''[[hispidosus]]'' for {{term|hispidus||rough, bristly}}, {{etyl|en|-}} ''[[hispid]]''.

{{etystub}]

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈhɪd.iː.ʌs/}} {{SAMPA|/"hId.i:.Vs/}}
* {{audio|en-us-hideous.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Frightful, shocking, or offensive to the eyes; dreadful to behold; as, a hideous monster; hideous looks.
#: A piteous and hideous spectacle." [[w:Thomas Babington Macaulay|Thomas Babington Macaulay]].
# Distressing or offensive to the ear; exciting terror or dismay; as, a hideous noise.
#: Hideous cries. {{Shak.}}
#* '''1719:''' [[w:Daniel Defoe|Daniel Defoe]], ''[[w:Robinson Crusoe|Robinson Crusoe]]''
#*: ''He started up, growling at first, but finding his leg broken, fell down again; and then got upon three legs, and gave the most '''hideous''' roar that ever I heard.''
# Hateful; shocking.
#: ''Sure, you have some hideous matter to deliver.'' - {{Shak.}}

